WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:13.330
<v Ira Glass>So many of us, we wander through this country. We wander through our lives. We wander in darkness. Often we feel lost. Like Chris. He was doing pretty badly. Living on the street. Hair to his shoulders.
<v Ira Glass>我们许多人都在这个国家流浪。我们在生活中流浪。我们在黑暗中流浪。我们常常感到迷茫。就像克里斯。他当时过得很糟。露宿街头。头发长到肩膀。

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:44.280
<v Chris Sewell>I called it the Unabomber look. I was just living in the woods. Just an open wooded area at the corner of Pond Drive and Route 347. I'd have fresh clothes on usually, but I only could do the wash every so often, and they were outside, so I smelled like the great outdoors, whether I liked that fact or not. And I very much knew I needed medical help.
<v Chris Sewell>我管它叫“大学炸弹客”造型。我就住在树林里。就在庞德大道和347号公路拐角处的一片开阔林地里。我通常会穿干净衣服，但我没法经常洗衣服，而且衣服是晾在外面的，所以我身上总有一股大自然的味道，不管我喜不喜欢。我非常清楚自己需要医疗救助。

00:00:44.280 --> 00:01:08.170
<v Ira Glass>So Chris needed this medical help, but of course, he had no money to pay for the medical help. He made money collecting cans and bottles on the street. Whenever he'd go to doctors or social services, he was so wacked-out looking that they wouldn't even talk to him like a person. They wouldn't even look him in the eye. It's a hell of a thing, isn't it? Wanting eye contact, not getting it. But then if you're lucky, somebody puts up the lights.
<v Ira Glass>克里斯需要医疗救助，但当然，他没钱支付。他靠在街上捡罐子和瓶子挣钱。每当他去找医生或社会服务部门时，他看起来总是那么落魄，以至于人们甚至不把他当人看。他们甚至不愿正眼看他。这真是见鬼，不是吗？渴望眼神交流，却得不到。但如果你走运，有人会为你点亮灯火。

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.620
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE CLAPPING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众鼓掌]

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:25.310
<v Ira Glass>Welcome. WBEZ Chicago. It's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Our program today recorded on a live tour of five cities: Boston, Washington DC, Portland, Oregon, Denver, and our hometown, Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎收听，芝加哥WBEZ电台。这里是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目是在五个城市的现场巡演中录制的：波士顿、华盛顿特区、俄勒冈州波特兰、丹佛，以及我们的家乡芝加哥。

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:33.370
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众欢呼]

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:49.350
<v Ira Glass>So Chris was stuck. He needed money to go to a doctor. And then he got this idea. He remembered how a couple years before that, he had wandered off the street into a shopping mall to get warm, and there the State of New York had set up two laptop computers on a folding table.
<v Ira Glass>所以克里斯陷入了困境。他需要钱去看医生。然后他想到了这个主意。他记得几年前，他曾从街上溜进一家购物中心取暖，在那里，纽约州在折叠桌上放了两台笔记本电脑。

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:57.030
<v Chris Sewell>And to see the lady just look at me, nod her head, and smile when I walked up to the booth, like I was just one of anybody, was great.
<v Chris Sewell>看到那位女士抬眼看我，点头微笑，就像我是一个普通人一样，那种感觉太棒了。

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:26.100
<v Ira Glass>So he sits down at the computers, and on these two computers you could look up your name and see whether the state was holding money for you that you did not know about. And there was all sorts of money. Utility bill refunds, tax refunds, lost checks, owed to all sorts of people and institutions. Lost money. Or not even lost, since most people didn't even know it was missing. This is like money in purgatory. This is money in limbo.
<v Ira Glass>他坐在电脑前，在这两台电脑上，你可以查询你的名字，看看州政府是否替你保管着你不知道的钱。钱的种类繁多。水电费退款、退税、丢失的支票，欠各种个人和机构的钱。丢失的钱。或者说甚至不算丢失，因为大多数人根本不知道这些钱不见了。这就像是在炼狱里的钱。这钱处于一种未定的状态。

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:40.930
<v Ira Glass>And thinking about all this, Chris Sewell realized how he could pay for his doctors. He would look online, find money of theirs that they did not know about, money that the state was holding for them, and then he would present that to them.
<v Ira Glass>想到这些，克里斯·塞维尔意识到他可以如何筹集看医生的钱。他可以在网上查询，找到那些他们自己都不知道的钱，那些州政府为他们保管的钱，然后把这些信息提供给他们。

00:02:40.930 --> 00:03:02.520
<v Chris Sewell>Well, I was hoping that I would suddenly get a couple of phone calls and a couple of checks, and people say, "oh, wow, thanks for having done this." And then I was hoping that these doctors would just even talk to me in a civil manner. And then work my way up from there.
<v Chris Sewell>我当时希望我能突然接到几个电话，收到几张支票，人们会说：“哦，哇，谢谢你做了这些。”然后我希望这些医生至少能以礼貌的态度和我说话。然后从那里开始改善我的生活。

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:30.580
<v Ira Glass>Now the crucial fact that I have to tell you about Chris Sewell at this point in the story is Chris is great at finding other people's money in these databases on the internet. And when he looked for money for the doctors at his local university hospital, not only did he find hundreds, hundreds of unclaimed Blue Cross Blue Shield checks, reimbursements, lost trust fund checks. He also found 100 checks that were owed to the hospital itself. It was a huge victory.
<v Ira Glass>现在我必须告诉你关于克里斯·塞维尔的一个关键事实，那就是克里斯非常擅长在互联网的这些数据库中找到别人的钱。当他为当地大学医院的医生查找资金时，他不仅找到了数百张未领取的蓝十字蓝盾支票、报销款、丢失的信托基金支票。他还发现了100张欠医院本身的支票。这是一场巨大的胜利。

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:37.730
<v Ira Glass>But when he went to the hospital to tell the doctors there that there was this money out there for them, he always got the same reaction.
<v Ira Glass>但当他去医院告诉那里的医生有这笔钱时，他总是得到同样的反应。

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:55.860
<v Chris Sewell>There was a total wall of silence. Except for the people who were telling me to buzz off. I actually went out and I printed out the claim forms. And I had a cover letter for it. And tried, in my own humble fashion, to appear professional. I went to the Salvation Army, bought myself a blue pinstripe suit. Even with my long hair and my beard, I went around and hand-delivered all of them.
<v Chris Sewell>那是一堵完全沉默的墙。除了那些叫我滚开的人。我实际上专门去打印了索赔表格。我还写了一封求职信。并以我卑微的方式，尽量让自己看起来专业。我去了救世军，给自己买了一套蓝色细条纹西装。即使留着长发和胡子，我也亲自去把它们送到了。

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.670
<v Ira Glass>And how did that work?
<v Ira Glass>结果怎么样？

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:07.660
<v Chris Sewell>That was the one that-- it didn't work. I didn't get responses. There was no conversation. As soon as I talked, security people would just appear out of the woodwork.
<v Chris Sewell>那是唯一一次——没成功。我没有得到回应。根本没有对话。只要我一开口，保安就会立刻出现。

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:33.700
<v Ira Glass>He tried letters. He tried emailing. Everybody just thought it was a scam. Eventually he got doctors to help him out through other means. And then he turned this talent elsewhere. He used it wherever and whenever he could. When his local public TV station was doing the pledge drive, he couldn't actually give money, of course, but he looked online, found $1,000 of theirs, called them up.
<v Ira Glass>他试过写信。他试过发邮件。每个人都以为那是骗局。最终，他通过其他途径获得了医生的帮助。然后他把这种天赋用到了别处。他尽可能地利用它。当当地公共电视台进行募捐活动时，他当然没钱捐赠，但他上网查询，找到了他们丢失的1000美元，并打电话告诉了他们。

00:04:33.700 --> 00:05:07.860
<v Ira Glass>In setting up this interview for our radio show, he was talking to one of our producers on the phone, Julie Snyder, and while he's on the phone with her, he found $400 bucks owed to WBEZ, the radio station where we work. And using nothing more than a normal internet connection, he found $610,940 owed to the City of New York. So much money that the city comptroller honored him with an old fashioned letter of commendation. Who knew that that was a real thing, and not just something that Commissioner Gordon would give to Batman now and then?
<v Ira Glass>在为我们的广播节目安排这次采访时，他正在电话里和我们的制片人朱莉·斯奈德交谈，就在和她通话时，他发现了WBEZ电台——我们工作的电台——欠下的400美元。仅仅利用普通的互联网连接，他找到了纽约市欠下的610,940美元。金额如此巨大，以至于市审计官授予他一封老式的表扬信。谁能想到这居然是真实存在的，而不只是戈登局长偶尔会发给蝙蝠侠的那种东西呢？

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:14.030
<v Ira Glass>And a press conference. It's impossible to overstate what that meant to him, that press conference.
<v Ira Glass>还有一场新闻发布会。那场发布会对他意味着什么，怎么形容都不为过。

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:29.760
<v Chris Sewell>I was extremely nervous. And there were big security lines. And I finally got there and walked into a room full of TV cameras. And I don't think I'd spoken to anybody before that in like two weeks.
<v Chris Sewell>我当时非常紧张。而且安保线排得很长。最后我终于到了，走进了挤满电视摄像机的房间。我想在那之前的两周里，我都没有和任何人说过话。

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.330
<v Ira Glass>You hadn't spoken with anybody at all for two weeks? For two weeks you hadn't had a conversation with another person?
<v Ira Glass>你已经两周没和任何人说过话了？两周没和别人交谈过？

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:52.340
<v Chris Sewell>Yeah. Again, I don't have a lot of friends where I live. I'm, you know-- trying to just reach out in the middle of a social void is very difficult. I mean, on the street, I think my record one time was I don't think I'd spoken to anybody for something like three months.
<v Chris Sewell>是的。再说一次，我住的地方没什么朋友。我，你知道——试图在社交空白中寻求联系是非常困难的。我是说，在街上时，我想我曾经最长的纪录是大概三个月没和任何人说过话。

00:05:52.340 --> 00:06:07.830
<v Chris Sewell>And I just-- I sort of tried to put a party face and soak it all in and radiate it back as best I could.
<v Chris Sewell>我只是——我尽量戴上一张社交的面孔，去感受这一切，并尽我所能地回馈。

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:23.760
<v Chris Sewell>The city comptroller actually recognized me. I feel that I've somehow helped create a place for myself in society, that I'm somehow a little less lost. I'm creating a purpose for my own life, and a direction.
<v Chris Sewell>市审计官真的认可了我。我觉得我以某种方式帮助自己在社会中建立了一席之地，觉得自己不再那么迷茫了。我正在为自己的人生创造一个目标，一个方向。

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:57.420
<v Ira Glass>Our program today: Lost in America. Stories of people who are lost, and how they sometimes, temporarily, if they're lucky, get found. On our program today, Sarah Vowell finds a terrorist, hiding and lost in a patriotic song. Jonathan Goldstein tries to lose something and finds that it is harder than it seems. And there is an entire magazine devoted to writing that people have lost, dropped, thrown away, and tried to destroy. Its creator reads samples on stage. Plus Jon Langford of the Mekons, the band OK Go and more. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目：《迷失在美国》。讲述人们迷失的故事，以及他们有时如何幸运地暂时被找回。在今天的节目中，萨拉·沃威尔发现了一个隐藏在爱国歌曲中、迷失的恐怖分子。乔纳森·戈尔茨坦试图丢失某样东西，却发现这比看起来要难。还有一本专门收集人们丢失、掉落、丢弃并试图销毁的文字的杂志。其创办人在现场朗读了选段。此外还有Mekons乐队的乔恩·兰福德、OK Go乐队等等。请继续收听。

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:06.670
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众欢呼]

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.480
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Losing It.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。失去它。

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:27.060
<v Ira Glass>Well, if you're going to do a program about people who are lost, you pretty much have to do a story about adolescence. Jonathan Goldstein is a regular contributor to our show. A quick warning before we begin, for parents listening with kids, he talks about sex in this story. Please welcome Jonathan Goldstein.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，如果你要制作一个关于迷失者的节目，你几乎必须做一个关于青春期的故事。乔纳森·戈尔茨坦是我们节目的常客。在开始之前给父母们提个醒，如果带孩子听，他在这个故事里谈到了性。请欢迎乔纳森·戈尔茨坦。

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:37.040
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众欢呼]

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:54.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Dying a virgin happened to people. Hans Christian Andersen, Queen Elizabeth the First. My grandmother's prophetically named brother, Uncle Hymen.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>处男之身而死的事确实发生过。安徒生、伊丽莎白一世女王。我祖母那个名字带有预言性的兄弟，海曼叔叔。

00:07:54.440 --> 00:08:21.280
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And I knew it was the kind of thing that could happen to me. Days would turn into months, months into years, and eventually I would drop dead in a men's hotel and be buried sexless in a single cemetery plot squeezed in on my side between my parents. And so I began, at an early age, what I thought of as my life's work, the work of not dying a virgin.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我知道这种事也可能发生在我身上。日子会变成月份，月份变成年份，最终我会死在一家廉价旅馆里，被葬在单一的墓地里，挤在父母墓地的侧面。于是，我在很小的时候就开始了我认为是我的人生使命，也就是不以处男之身死去的工作。

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:49.000
<v Jonathan Goldstein>At 10 I met Varid, my first crush. She was in from Israel with her family, and was staying with our neighbors. Since this was the summer Grease came out, I invited Varid upstairs to our living room, where I played the soundtrack on our stereo. While it played, I danced for her. My feeling was that you had to give girls everything you had-- your showmanship, your best moves, even your dignity.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>10岁时，我遇到了瓦里德，我的初恋。她和家人从以色列来，住在我们邻居家。由于那年夏天《油脂》上映了，我邀请瓦里德到我们客厅，在那里我用音响播放了原声带。音乐响起时，我为她跳舞。我的感觉是，你必须给女孩你所拥有的一切——你的表演才能，你最好的舞步，甚至你的尊严。

00:08:49.000 --> 00:09:12.200
<v Jonathan Goldstein>As the music played, Varid sat there with quiet indifference. At one point, I tried to bring her soul to life by lying down on our coffee table and spinning around on my stomach like a spastic Lazy Susan. When I did the splits, I fell to the ground with a ferocity that almost tore my scrotal sac in two.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>音乐播放时，瓦里德坐在那里，带着安静的冷漠。有一次，我试图通过躺在咖啡桌上，像一个痉挛的懒人餐桌一样在肚子上旋转来点燃她的灵魂。当我做劈叉时，我猛地摔倒在地，差点把我的阴囊撕成两半。

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:45.210
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Before Varid went back to Israel, I handed her all 10 Archie digests that I owned. I stacked them so all the spines lined up perfectly. I wanted them to look solemn and substantial, like a package wrapped in rope from the old country. "It's how I feel about you," I wanted to say, but her English was not good enough to understand. For the next several years, I would say some variation of these words to many different people, but no one's English was ever good enough to understand.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>瓦里德回以色列之前，我给了她我拥有的全部10本《阿奇》文摘。我把它们叠好，书脊对齐。我想让它们看起来庄重而厚实，就像一捆从老家带出来的用绳子包好的包裹。“这是我对你的感觉，”我想这么说，但她的英语水平不足以理解。在接下来的几年里，我会对许多不同的人说这些话的变体，但没有人的英语好到能听懂。

00:09:45.210 --> 00:10:20.010
<v Jonathan Goldstein>At the age of 13, I entered the service industry. The self service industry. I spent all of my time listening to music like Yellow while fantasizing about the high school gym teacher play tether ball in her brassiere. Or Stevie Nicks strumming a guitar in her brassiere. Or in my darker moments, Golda Meir ruling a nation in her brassiere.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>13岁时，我进入了服务行业。自我服务行业。我所有的时间都在听像《黄色》这样的音乐，同时幻想着高中体育老师穿着胸罩打拍球。或者史蒂薇·妮克丝穿着胸罩弹吉他。或者在我更阴暗的时刻，果尔达·梅厄穿着胸罩统治一个国家。

00:10:20.010 --> 00:11:01.700
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And then at 14 our family got one of those early model elephantine video machines. I rented early 80s teen sex comedies like Private Lessons, Private School for Girls, and Reform School Girl. I would make each 20-second shower scene last anywhere from six to seven hours. Pause, rewind, frame advance became my version of the holy trinity. One time, having lost the remote control to our VCR, I watched the entirety of Sorority Girls on Vacation lying on the basement floor with the VCR on my chest, hitting its buttons like a 70 pound accordion.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>然后在14岁时，我们家有了那款早期型号的巨型录像机。我租了80年代早期的青少年性喜剧，像《私人课程》、《女子私立学校》和《感化院女孩》。我会把每一个20秒的淋浴场景播放个六七个小时。暂停、倒带、逐帧播放成了我的三位一体。有一次，因为弄丢了录像机的遥控器，我躺在地下室的地板上，把录像机放在胸口，像手风琴一样按着它的按钮。

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:34.290
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It was around that time, inspired by a potent cocktail of sexual desperation and movies starring Phoebe Cates, that I left the comfort of home for the streets. A 4 foot, 10 inch, pheromone-drenched Cabbage Patch doll with acne on the prowl. And thus at 16, I met Tamara. Everyone had a locker partner at our school, and I shared mine with Tamara. Just knowing that no matter what, our winter boots were alone in the dark together for six hours a day was enough to make me feel at least that something in my life was going all right.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>就在那时，受到性绝望和菲比·凯茨主演的电影的刺激，我离开了舒适的家，走向街头。一个4英尺10英寸、充满荷尔蒙、长满痘痘的卷心菜娃娃在出没。于是，在16岁时，我遇到了塔玛拉。学校里的每个人都有储物柜搭档，我和塔玛拉共用一个。只要知道无论如何，我们的冬靴每天有六个小时在黑暗中待在一起，就足以让我觉得我的人生至少有一点进展。

00:11:34.290 --> 00:12:07.370
<v Jonathan Goldstein>One day I finally worked up the courage to ask her if she wanted to see the Eurythmics concert with me, because I knew she was a big fan, and she said yes. I don't know where I came up with the idea, but I had decided at some point during the concert that I wanted to hold Tamara from behind and sway with her back and forth to the music. It was all I could think about all night. It wasn't a kiss or a held hand I was after, but something else, something less definable. It was like I wanted to prove that I could get us into some kind of groove.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有一天，我终于鼓起勇气问她想不想和我一起去看Eurythmics演唱会，因为我知道她是他们的超级粉丝，她答应了。我不知道这个想法是从哪儿冒出来的，但在演唱会期间的某个时刻，我决定我想从背后抱着塔玛拉，随着音乐和她一起摇摆。整晚我满脑子都是这个。我追求的不是吻或牵手，而是别的东西，一些更难定义的东西。我就好像想证明我能把我们带入某种节奏。

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:34.900
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Finally, during a slow song, I snuck up behind her and put my sweaty pocket hands on her hips. I tried to ease us into it. My movements were halted and completely non-musical. Rather than dancing, it looked like I was trying to hoist a resistant woman into a mailbox while in the throes of a painful piles attack. Tamara freed herself, and for the rest of the evening, she was afraid to come near me.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>最终，在一首慢歌中，我溜到她身后，把汗津津的手放在她的臀部上。我试图让我们进入状态。我的动作很生硬，完全没有音乐感。与其说是跳舞，看起来倒更像是在痔疮发作的剧痛中试图把一个反抗的女人塞进邮箱。塔玛拉挣脱了，那天晚上剩下的时间里，她都不敢靠近我。

00:12:34.900 --> 00:13:08.910
<v Jonathan Goldstein>At home that night I ate a bowl of cold cereal was staring at a bottle of my mother's nail polish on the kitchen counter. Carefully I painted my nails. In bed, my fingers spider-walked the length of my ribs. "Quit tickling me," I told myself. "I have to make this an early night." I grabbed my hand and flung it away from myself roughly. but it always crept back.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天晚上在家，我吃了一碗冷麦片，盯着厨房柜台上我母亲的指甲油瓶。我小心翼翼地涂了指甲。在床上，我的手指像蜘蛛一样爬过肋骨。“别挠我，”我对自己说。“我得早点睡。”我抓住自己的手，粗暴地甩开。但它总是又爬了回来。

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:37.360
<v Jonathan Goldstein>If early 80s teen sex comedies had taught me anything, which, unfortunately, they had in abundance, it was that college was going to be a nonstop nookie fest. I wasn't fat enough to be the one who sees undressing sorority girls shortly before falling backwards from a ladder. Nor was I daring enough to have sex with a friend's mom. Nor was I near sighted and good enough at science to create a fellatio bot.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如果80年代早期的青少年性喜剧教会了我什么（不幸的是，它们确实教会了我很多），那就是大学将是一场永无止境的性爱派对。我没胖到那种会在看更衣室女孩换衣服时从梯子上向后摔倒的笨蛋程度。我也没胆量和朋友的妈妈发生关系。我也没近视到能创造出一个口交机器人的科学天才程度。

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:55.920
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But I did know that I was the nice guy, the one that drove everyone around on their way to getting action, and that before the credits rolled, I would receive my due. It was only right. Well, the credits had rolled, the curtain had come down, there was a man sweeping up popcorn, and still no due.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但我确实知道我是那个好人，那个在送别人去搞对象时负责开车的人，而且在片尾字幕滚动之前，我会得到我应得的回报。这是理所当然的。好吧，字幕滚动了，幕布拉下了，有个男人在扫爆米花，而我还是没得到应得的。

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:23.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>For winter break, my friend Howard and I decided to spend the week in New York City. As we rode the train there, we passed a burned out school bus in a field, and I thought, yes, that is my heart. My heart is a burned out school bus with no windows sitting in the middle of nowhere. But then after about 15 minutes, the train passed a little gray wooden control booth, the door flung wide open, and I thought, no, that. That is my heart.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>寒假期间，我和朋友霍华德决定在纽约市待上一周。当我们乘火车去那里时，我们经过田野里一辆烧毁的校车，我想，是的，那就是我的心。我的心是一辆烧毁的、没有窗户的校车，停在虚无之中。但大约15分钟后，火车经过一个小小的灰色木质控制亭，门敞开着，我想，不，是那个。那才是我的心。

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:39.780
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The train continued along through February fields, and I looked for more things to call my heart. A rusty bridge. A chopped down evergreen. A discarded can of Mountain Dew. It turned out my heart was everywhere that winter month.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>火车继续穿过二月的田野，我寻找更多可以称为我心的东西。一座生锈的桥。一棵被砍倒的常青树。一个丢弃的山露汽水罐。结果那天冬天，我的心无处不在。

00:14:39.780 --> 00:15:07.390
<v Jonathan Goldstein>We stayed with Howard's aunt in Flushing, and every night we would go to the local bar and take pictures of ourselves scribbling in our notebooks, our faces illuminated by the neon jukebox. It was at that bar that we met a 25-year-old Venezuelan furniture salesman named Jose. Howard and I thought Jose was the coolest. While we drank our rum and Coca-Cola's, Jose drank bloody caesars, something we considered very sophisticated, sort of like drinking a salad.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我们住在霍华德在法拉盛的姑妈家，每天晚上我们都会去当地的酒吧，拍下自己在笔记本上乱涂乱画的照片，我们的脸被霓虹灯点唱机照亮。就在那家酒吧里，我们遇到了一个25岁的委内瑞拉家具销售员，名叫何塞。霍华德和我觉得何塞酷毙了。当我们喝着朗姆可乐时，何塞喝着血腥凯撒，我们认为这非常高级，有点像是在喝沙拉。

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:33.740
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Howard explained his dilemma to Jose, and I explained mine. Howard had had cancer when he was 16 and was concerned he wouldn't ever be able to produce semen and have children. I explained that I had grown weary of being a virgin and really, really wanted to see naked chimichangas. Jose furrowed his brow and listened to our problems with concern. Then he said he was taking us to a whore house.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>霍华德向何塞解释了他的困境，我也解释了我的。霍华德16岁时患过癌症，担心自己永远无法产生精子，生儿育女。我解释说我厌倦了做处男，非常、非常想看裸体的墨西哥卷饼。何塞皱着眉头，关切地听着我们的问题。然后他说他要带我们去妓院。

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:57.580
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I cottoned to the idea of a whore house by telling myself that I was very old school, very Neil Simon, or Burt Reynolds, or something. It was probably how my father and his father before him did it. I imagined my grandfather had lost his virginity to a kind-hearted madam, who, after the deed, had cooked him a brisket.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我接受了去妓院的想法，因为我告诉自己，我非常老派，非常尼尔·西蒙，或者是伯特·雷诺兹之流。我父亲和他父亲之前可能也是这么做的。我想象着我的祖父是在一位心地善良的老鸨那里失去了童贞，她在完事后还给他做了一份牛腩。

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:21.480
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Jose took us to an apartment above a fruit store in Spanish Harlem, where we were invited in by a small, older Latino man wearing a shoulder holster. Jose told us not to worry, that this is how things were done. We were seated on a couch facing an East Indian man and what appeared to be his two teenage sons. There were three women dressed in nighties who walked by carrying balled-up faded linen and punch bowl-sized metal basins with soapy sponges.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>何塞带我们去了西班牙哈莱姆区一家水果店楼上的公寓，一个穿着肩挂式枪套、身材矮小的老拉丁裔男人邀请我们进去。何塞告诉我们不用担心，事情就是这样办的。我们坐在沙发上，对面坐着一个印度男人和他看起来像他两个十几岁的儿子的男孩。有三个穿着睡裙的女人走过，手里拿着揉成一团的褪色亚麻布和装有肥皂水的碗状金属盆。

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:47.720
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It was after seeing the basins that I realized I would never be able to pull this off. There was something too medicinal and matter-of-fact about the whole thing. Not only that, but I was terrified. Sex was one thing, but if I was going to be getting naked on a strange bed with sheets that were dubious, I at least wanted to be with someone who mildly liked me. And that, of course, is something impossible to gauge when the exchange of Canadian travelers cheques is involved.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>看到那些盆子后，我意识到我永远无法完成这件事。整个过程有一种过于医疗化和实事求是的氛围。不仅如此，我还吓坏了。性是一回事，但如果我要在一个床单可疑的陌生床上裸体，我至少希望和一个稍微喜欢我的人在一起。而这，当然是在涉及兑换加拿大旅行支票时无法衡量的事情。

00:16:47.720 --> 00:17:05.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I told Jose I would sit this one out. Howard, on the other hand, was raring to go. A woman came over to him and introduced herself as Crystal. Howard introduced himself as Mr. Cohenovsky, which was the name of our grade seven math teacher. Crystal took Howard by the hand and led him into a room.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我告诉何塞我要退出。另一方面，霍华德却跃跃欲试。一个女人走过来，介绍自己叫克里斯特尔。霍华德介绍自己是科恩诺夫斯基先生，那是我们七年级数学老师的名字。克里斯特尔牵起霍华德的手，把他领进了一个房间。

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:31.240
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Each person got 20 minutes. Howard remained in the room with Crystal for 40. The Indian man sitting opposite me kept pointing to his watch while smiling and winking. There's nothing that makes you think about dying a virgin more than sitting on the couch in a whore house waiting for your best friend to finish having sex. That couch just then felt like the loneliest place on earth.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>每个人20分钟。霍华德和克里斯特尔在房间里待了40分钟。坐在我斜对面的那个印度男人不停地指着他的手表，同时对我微笑眨眼。没有什么比坐在妓院的沙发上等最好的朋友完事更让人想起死于处男之身的了。那一刻，那张沙发感觉像是地球上最孤独的地方。

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:51.530
<v Jonathan Goldstein>As it turned out, Crystal saw Howard's scar, and they spent most of their time together talking about what it was like to have had cancer. Crystal had lost a brother to leukemia several years earlier. She told Howard that when her and her brother were kids, they poked toothpicks into bubble gum, which they ate like hors d'oeuvres. She told Howard that she missed her brother very much.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>事实证明，克里斯特尔看到了霍华德的伤疤，他们在一起的大部分时间都在谈论患过癌症是什么感觉。克里斯特尔几年前因白血病失去了弟弟。她告诉霍华德，当她和弟弟小时候，他们会把牙签插在泡泡糖上吃，就像吃开胃小菜一样。她告诉霍华德她非常想念弟弟。

00:17:51.530 --> 00:18:05.000
<v Jonathan Goldstein>On the walk back to the subway, I lagged behind Howard, just watching him. Eventually we asked Howard how it was with Crystal, and he said that it was different than he thought it would be. And Jose told him he was being too emotional.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>走回地铁的路上，我落后在霍华德身后，只是看着他。最终我们问霍华德和克里斯特尔在一起感觉如何，他说这和预想的不一样。何塞告诉他太情绪化了。

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:27.650
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What I hadn't taken into account was that before you lose something-- in this case, the albatross of one's virginity-- you had to first find something to enable that loss. At 18, sort of by accident, I found Galeet. Her name meant, "little wave," and when we kissed, it was like a little wave of blood was sweeping through my chest. We kissed long and hard, our eyes wide open.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我没有考虑到的事情是，在你失去某样东西之前——在这个例子中，是处男之身的枷锁——你必须先找到某种能让这种丧失发生的东西。18岁时，某种程度上是偶然，我找到了加莉特。她的名字意为“小浪”，当我们接吻时，就像一股小浪涌过我的胸膛。我们吻得很长很深，眼睛睁得大大的。

00:18:27.650 --> 00:19:03.110
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Galeet's face was always sad. I told her she had a frown that lit up a room. Galeet would be the first person I would ever sleep with, yet losing my virginity with her, even just after it happened, was something I can never remember. I could still recall what her breath smelled like after she had eaten chocolate, how she would almost cry when she saw little boys with big glasses. I remember a thousand other small things, too, yet our first time together, this thing that I waited forever to happen, to lose a virginity I had waited forever to lose, was completely and utterly lost to me.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>加莉特的脸上总带着忧伤。我告诉她，她的眉头能照亮整个房间。加莉特是我第一个发生关系的女人，然而与她失去童贞，即使就在刚刚发生之后，也是我永远无法回忆起来的事情。我仍然记得她吃过巧克力后呼吸的味道，记得她看到戴着大眼镜的小男孩时几乎要哭出来的样子。我还记得一千件其他的小事，然而我们第一次在一起，这个我等待了永远才发生的事情，要失去我等待了永远才失去的童贞，对我来说却完全彻底地消失了。

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:23.780
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I'm not sure I understand why this is. Maybe when you're coming towards it, it looks so big and all-eclipsing. It looks like the finish line. You imagine champagne, well wishers, certificates of merit. Then you reach it, and pass it, and you understand it's only the entry point to a lot of other worries, worries that prove to be much more immeasurable and complicated.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我不确定为什么会这样。也许当你接近它时，它看起来是那么巨大而遮天蔽日。它看起来像是终点线。你想象着香槟、祝福者、优异证书。然后你到达了它，越过了它，你才明白它仅仅是通向许多其他烦恼的入口，那些烦恼被证明要复杂和无法衡量得多。

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:38.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>My fear of losing my virginity was nothing compared to my fear of losing Galeet. I could never fathom how she could love me, and so I tested her love all the time. "Let's say I smashed your car," I said.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我对失去童贞的恐惧，与我对失去加莉特的恐惧相比根本不算什么。我永远无法理解她怎么能爱我，所以我一直在测试她的爱。“假设我撞坏了你的车，”我说。

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:41.530
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"Then I'd kill you," she said.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“那我杀了你，”她说。

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:43.850
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"But you'd still love me?"
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“但你还会爱我吗？”

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:47.340
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"I guess so," she said.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“我想会吧，”她说。

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:59.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Before I met Galeet, I called myself a virgin. It was very clear and definite-- a virgin. Now there is no name for what I am. But I'm sort of partial to Leroy.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在遇到加莉特之前，我称自己为处男。这很明确，很确定——一个处男。现在我已经没有名字了。但我有点喜欢“勒罗伊”这个名字。

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:01.040
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Thanks.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>谢谢。

