00:22:50.180 --> 00:23:07.030
<v Sarah Vowell>At the memorial service at the National Cathedral on September 14, 2001, those gathered in the pews to mourn the more than 2,800 people who were murdered by terrorists three days before sang "The Battle Hymn of The Republic."
<v Sarah Vowell>2001年9月14日，在国家大教堂举行的纪念仪式上，聚集在长椅上哀悼三天前被恐怖分子谋杀的2800多名受害者的人们唱起了《共和国战歌》。

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:30.890
<v Sarah Vowell>[MUSIC - "BATTLE HYMN OF THE REPUBLIC" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND] Mine eyes have seen the glory of the coming of the Lord: He is trampling out the vintage where the grapes of wrath are stored; He hath loosed the fateful lightning of His terrible swift sword: His truth is marching on.
<v Sarah Vowell>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“共和国战歌”] 我的双眼见证了主降临的荣光：他正踩踏着盛满愤怒葡萄的酒槽；他释放了可怕迅捷的闪电之剑：他的真理永向前进。

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:57.650
<v Ira Glass>President Bush sang it. Ex-presidents Clinton, Carter, and Bush sang it. Watching it on television in New York City, I sang it. And across the sea at another service in another church, Queen Elizabeth sang it with tears in her English eyes, having sung the song 36 years earlier at the funeral of Winston Churchill, as Churchill himself had sung it along with FDR on the White House lawn in 1943.
<v Ira Glass>布什总统唱了它。前总统克林顿、卡特和布什都唱了。我在纽约的电视上看着它，我唱了。而在大洋彼岸的另一座教堂的另一个仪式上，伊丽莎白女王含着泪水唱了它，她曾在36年前温斯顿·丘吉尔的葬礼上唱过这首歌，正如丘吉尔本人在1943年与罗斯福总统在白宫草坪上一起唱的那样。

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:09.720
<v Ira Glass>Because that's when we sing "The Battle Hymn of the Republic," at wars and funerals. So "The Battle Hymn" was doubly appropriate on September 14. It was a funeral, we were at war.
<v Ira Glass>因为那就是我们唱《共和国战歌》的时候，在战争和葬礼上。所以《战歌》在9月14日是双重适宜的。这是一场葬礼，我们正处于战争之中。

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:32.480
<v Ira Glass>And yet there we were, we gathered Americans, her Majesty the queen, chanting this beloved battle cry at the terrorists of the world. And buried inside the melody of the song was the rotting corpse of an American terrorist. Here are the lyrics that used to go to that tune back in 1860, lyrics that honor the terrorist as a hero.
<v Ira Glass>然而我们就在那里，聚集的美国人，女王陛下，向世界上的恐怖分子高唱这首深受喜爱的战斗口号。而埋藏在这首歌的旋律之中的，是一个美国恐怖分子的腐烂尸体。这是1860年那首歌原本的歌词，歌词将那个恐怖分子尊为英雄。

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:49.410
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "JOHN BROWN'S BODY" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND] John brown's body lies moldering in the grave, John Brown's body lies moldering in the grave. John Brown's body lies moldering in the grave; his soul goes marching on.
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“约翰·布朗之躯”] 约翰·布朗的身体躺在坟墓中腐烂，约翰·布朗的身体躺在坟墓中腐烂。约翰·布朗的身体躺在坟墓中腐烂；他的灵魂依然前进。

00:24:49.410 --> 00:25:29.630
<v Ira Glass>I don't throw around the word terrorist lightly, but if it's John Brown-- Brown, a white man who hated slavery so much that even his friend, the famed abolitionist and former slave Frederick Douglass, wished Brown would shut up about it every now and then. Back in 1856, Brown and his men massacred five pro-slavery settlers from the south near Pottawatomie Creek, Kansas, dragging them from their homes as their wives and children looked on screaming. They hacked them up with knives, though Brown shot one man's mangled body in the head just to make certain he was dead for sure.
<v Ira Glass>我不会轻易使用“恐怖分子”这个词，但如果那是约翰·布朗——布朗，一个憎恨奴隶制到连他的朋友、著名废奴主义者和前奴隶弗雷德里克·道格拉斯都希望他偶尔闭嘴的白人。早在1856年，布朗和他的部下在堪萨斯州的波塔瓦托米溪附近屠杀了五名来自南方的支持奴隶制的定居者，在他们的妻子和孩子尖叫围观下，将他们从家中拖出。他们用刀将他们砍死，尽管布朗又对着其中一具血肉模糊的尸体的头部开了一枪，只是为了确定他确实死透了。

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:50.180
<v Ira Glass>Then hoping to incite a slave uprising, Brown led a guerrilla attack on the Federal Arsenal at Harper's Ferry, Virginia on October 16, 1859. 17 people lost their lives, including 10 of Brown's men, two of whom were his sons. Brown was executed on December 2, 1859.
<v Ira Glass>然后为了煽动奴隶起义，布朗于1859年10月16日对弗吉尼亚州哈珀斯费里的联邦军火库发动了游击袭击。17人丧生，包括布朗的10名部下，其中两人是他的儿子。布朗于1859年12月2日被处决。

00:25:50.180 --> 00:26:34.490
<v Ira Glass>Across the land, Brown's execution was either celebrated or mourned with the ringing church bells, depending on which side of the Mason Dixon Line one hung one's heart. In Concord, Massachusetts, Brown's friends, including Ralph Waldo Emerson, held a service in which Henry David Thoreau called Brown a crucified hero. Thoreau was so excited about Brown's martyrdom to the abolitionist cause, that he cheered that Brown's death gave people who were contemplating suicide, quote, "something to live for," exclamation point. And, as Lincoln puts it in his second inaugural, "the war came."
<v Ira Glass>在全国范围内，布朗的处决要么被庆祝，要么在教堂钟声中被哀悼，这取决于一个人的心挂在哪一边。在马萨诸塞州的康科德，包括拉尔夫·沃尔多·爱默生在内的布朗的朋友们举行了一场仪式，亨利·大卫·梭罗称布朗为受难的英雄。梭罗对布朗为废奴事业殉难感到如此兴奋，以至于他欢呼道，布朗的死给了那些考虑自杀的人，“活下去的理由”，感叹号。而且，正如林肯在第二次就职演说中所说，“战争来了”。

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:56.260
<v Ira Glass>Then John Brown got a song of his own, sort of. Like every turn in this story, the song "John Brown's Body" is an accident of history. What happened was Union soldiers-- the 12th Massachusetts Regiment-- were stationed at Fort Warren in Boston Harbor. As they went about their duties, the soldiers sang. One of their favorites was this Methodist hymn:
<v Ira Glass>然后约翰·布朗有了属于他自己的歌，某种程度上。就像这个故事里的每一个转折点，歌曲“约翰·布朗之躯”是历史的一个意外。事情是这样的：联邦军队——马萨诸塞第12团——驻扎在波士顿港的沃伦堡。当他们履行职责时，士兵们唱歌。他们最喜欢的歌曲之一是这首卫理公会赞美诗：

00:26:56.260 --> 00:27:02.300
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SAY, BROTHERS, WON'T YOU MEET US" BY JON LANGFORD] Say, brother, will you meet us on Canaan's happy shore?
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德的“兄弟们，你们愿意在迦南海岸迎接我们吗”] 兄弟们，你们愿意在迦南的幸福海岸迎接我们吗？

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:31.610
<v Ira Glass>There was a singing quartet in residence at the fort: Sergeant Charles Edgerly, Sergeant Newton J. Purnette, Sergeant James Jenkins, and Sergeant John Brown. One day in December of 1859, news arrived at the fort about the famous abolitionist. John Brown is dead. Some smart alec, thinking of his singing comrade Sergeant John Brown, is said to have replied, "but he still goes marching around."
<v Ira Glass>堡垒里有一个驻扎的歌唱四重奏组：查尔斯·埃杰利中士、牛顿·J·普内特中士、詹姆斯·詹金斯中士和约翰·布朗中士。1859年12月的一天，关于那位著名废奴主义者的消息传到了堡垒。约翰·布朗死了。一些聪明人，想到他的歌唱同伴约翰·布朗中士，据说回答说：“但他依然在前进。”

00:27:31.610 --> 00:28:08.100
<v Ira Glass>A soldier named Henry Howgreen reportedly turned this wise crack into the first verse of the song about his comrade, "John Brown's body lies moldering in the grave, his soul goes marching on." A soldier who played the organ set it to the music of "Say, Brothers, Won't You Meet Us." So the song was a joke, joshing among soldiers, the sort of ribbing a sergeant in today's army might get if he had the misfortune of being named Uday Hussein.
<v Ira Glass>一名名叫亨利·豪格林的士兵据称把这个笑话变成了关于他同伴的歌的第一节：“约翰·布朗的身体躺在坟墓中腐烂，他的灵魂依然前进。”一位弹风琴的士兵将它配上了“兄弟们，你们愿意迎接我们吗”的曲子。所以这首歌是一个玩笑，士兵们之间的调侃，就像今天军队里的中士如果不幸被取名为乌代·侯赛因可能会遭受的那种揶揄。

00:28:08.100 --> 00:28:34.090
<v Ira Glass>"John Brown's Body" became the regiment's marching song. They sang in public for the first time, here in Boston on State Street, on July 18, 1861. After that, they sang it marching down Broadway in New York City. They sang it so often they became known as The Hallelujah Regiment. On March 1, 1862, they sang in Virginia on the very spot the abolitionist John Brown was hanged.
<v Ira Glass>“约翰·布朗之躯”成了该团的行军歌曲。1861年7月18日，他们第一次在波士顿州街公开演唱。之后，他们在纽约百老汇行军时也唱了它。他们唱得如此频繁，以至于被称为“哈利路亚团”。1862年3月1日，他们在弗吉尼亚州演唱，就在那位废奴主义者约翰·布朗被绞死的地方。

00:28:34.090 --> 00:29:06.670
<v Ira Glass>Three months later, Sergeant John Brown drowned while crossing the Shenandoah River on his way to battle. Heartbroken, The Hallelujah Regiment never sang "John Brown's Body" again. But by that time, they were the only ones in the Union Army not singing it. Northern soldiers would sing it on parade, marching from battle to battle, sitting around camp fires at night. And since no one had ever heard of Sergeant Brown, every soldier who sang the song thought he was paying tribute to the famed abolitionist.
<v Ira Glass>三个月后，约翰·布朗中士在横渡谢南多厄河参加战斗时淹死了。伤心欲绝的“哈利路亚团”再也没有唱过“约翰·布朗之躯”。但到那时，他们已经是联邦军队中唯一不唱这首歌的人了。北方士兵在阅兵时、从一场战斗行军到另一场战斗时、晚上在营火旁都会唱这首歌。而且由于没有人听说过布朗中士，每个唱这首歌的士兵都认为他是在向那位著名的废奴主义者致敬。

00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:13.620
<v Ira Glass>Lyrics kept getting added on, including one doozy of a stanza about the president of the confederacy.
<v Ira Glass>歌词不断被添加，包括关于邦联总统的一节荒唐的歌词。

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:30.490
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "JOHN BROWN'S BODY" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND] We'll hang Jeff Davis to a sour apple tree, we'll hang Jeff Davis to a sour apple tree. We'll hang Jeff Davis to a sour apple tree; as we go marching on.
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“约翰·布朗之躯”] 我们要把杰夫·戴维斯吊在酸苹果树上，要把杰夫·戴维斯吊在酸苹果树上，要把杰夫·戴维斯吊在酸苹果树上；我们继续前进。

00:29:30.490 --> 00:30:00.780
<v Ira Glass>In November of 1861, some white abolitionists from Boston, including Mr. and Mrs. Samuel Gridley Howe, and their minister Reverend James F. Clark, were touring a Union army base on the outskirts of the nation's capital. They heard some soldiers singing about John Brown's body "moldering in the grave." Reverend Clark turned to Mrs. Howe and wondered if she might like to write new, more uplifting lyrics to that fine melody of "John Brown's Body."
<v Ira Glass>1861年11月，来自波士顿的一些白人废奴主义者，包括塞缪尔·格里德利·豪夫妇和他们的牧师詹姆斯·F·克拉克，正在参观国家首都郊外的一个联邦陆军基地。他们听到一些士兵唱着约翰·布朗的身体“在坟墓中腐烂”。克拉克牧师转向豪夫人，想知道她是否愿意为“约翰·布朗之躯”那优美的旋律写下新的、更令人振奋的歌词。

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:17.500
<v Ira Glass>Not that Julia Ward Howe had a problem with John Brown. She had even hosted Brown at dinner in her home on one of his trips to Massachusetts, trying to drum up money he could use smiting the slave mongers. Basically, abolitionist pledge drive.
<v Ira Glass>并不是说朱莉娅·沃德·豪对约翰·布朗有什么意见。她甚至在马萨诸塞州的一次旅行中在家里接待过布朗，试图帮他筹钱去打击奴隶贩子。基本上就是废奴主义者的募捐活动。

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.100
<v Ira Glass>You are the public in public hanging.
<v Ira Glass>你是公开绞刑中的公众。

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:26.260
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[观众笑声]

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:58.670
<v Ira Glass>Julia Ward Howe would recall of the morning she wrote new lyrics that she had "the feeling that something of importance had happened to me." Back home in Boston, she gave the poem to her neighbor, James T. Fields, editor of the Atlantic Monthly. He paid Howe $4.00, gave her poem the title, "The Battle Hymn of the Republic," and published it in the February 1862 issue. "The Battle Hymn" became a huge hit. Who knew the Atlantic Monthly was the TRL of its day?
<v Ira Glass>朱莉娅·沃德·豪在回忆写下新歌词的那天早晨时，说她有“一种重要的事情发生在我身上的感觉”。回到波士顿，她把这首诗给了邻居詹姆斯·T·菲尔兹，他是《大西洋月刊》的编辑。他付给豪4美元，给她的诗起了个名字叫《共和国战歌》，并发表在1862年2月号上。《战歌》成了巨大的成功。谁知道《大西洋月刊》是当时的MTV热歌排行榜呢？

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:10.990
<v Ira Glass>Soldiers and citizens alike couldn't stop singing it. When Abraham Lincoln heard it at a rally, it is said that his eyes filled with tears and he yelled, "sing it again."
<v Ira Glass>士兵和公民们都无法停止传唱它。当亚伯拉罕·林肯在集会上听到它时，据说他的双眼盈满了泪水，大喊道：“再唱一遍。”

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:12.060
<v Ira Glass>Sing it again.
<v Ira Glass>再唱一遍。

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:34.690
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE BATTLE HYMN OF THE REPUBLIC" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND] I have read a fiery gospel writ in burnished rows of steel: "As ye deal with my contemners, so with you my grace shall deal; let the hero born of woman crush the serpent with his heel, since God is marching on."
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“共和国战歌”] 我读过用钢铁烧制的炽热福音：“既然你们对待我的蔑视者，那么我的恩典也会那样对待你们；让妇女所生的英雄用脚跟粉碎那条毒蛇，因为上帝依然前进。”

00:31:34.690 --> 00:32:28.430
<v Ira Glass>That line in the hymn about the hero born of woman, which is a shout-out to the Virgin Mary by way of William Shakespeare, takes on an extra meaning in light of Julia Ward Howe's biography. Though she was married for 34 years, raised six children, and is, in fact, the creator of Mother's Day, Julia how was not without ambivalence about domesticity. For example, she wrote a poem entitled "The Present is Dead. " On her honeymoon. In writing to her sister about what she called "the stupor of marriage and motherhood," Howe complained, "it has been like blindness, like death, like exile from all things beautiful and good."
<v Ira Glass>赞美诗中关于“妇女所生的英雄”的那一行，是对威廉·莎士比亚笔下圣母玛利亚的致敬，在朱莉娅·沃德·豪的生平背景下有了额外的含义。虽然她结婚34年，抚养了六个孩子，并且实际上还是“母亲节”的创造者，但朱莉娅·豪对家庭主妇的生活并非没有矛盾。例如，她在度蜜月时写了一首题为《现在已死》的诗。在给姐妹写信描述她所谓的“婚姻和母亲身份的昏迷”时，豪抱怨道：“这就像失明，像死亡，像被放逐出所有美好和善良的事物。”

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:47.580
<v Ira Glass>So in "The Battle Hymn of the Republic," Howe reminds the nations that the Son of God had a human mom who did all the work. Hence, "let the hero born of woman crush the serpent with his heel," Howe is rubbing in the fact that even Jesus had to be potty trained.
<v Ira Glass>所以在《共和国战歌》中，豪提醒各国，上帝之子有一位做尽所有工作的凡人母亲。因此，“让妇女所生的英雄用脚跟粉碎那条毒蛇”，豪在提醒人们，甚至连耶稣也得学习如厕训练。

00:32:47.580 --> 00:33:07.220
<v Ira Glass>Like a lot of your bigger hit songs, "The Battle Hymn" has been ripped off numerous times. Countless renditions have been sent to its tune, which is fair enough considering that Julia Ward Howe ripped off a song which was a rip off of a previous song. There was "Solidarity Forever," a union number from the IWW songbook.
<v Ira Glass>像很多热门歌曲一样，《战歌》被翻唱过无数次。它的旋律被用过无数次，考虑到朱莉娅·沃德·豪窃取了一首歌，而那首歌本身又是对之前一首歌的窃取，这倒也公平。还有“团结永远”，来自IWW歌集的一首工会歌曲。

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:24.960
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SOLIDARITY FOREVER" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND] When the union's inspiration through the workers' blood shall run, there can be no greater anywhere beneath the sun; yet what force on earth weaker than the feeble strength of one, for the union makes us strong.
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“团结永远”] 当工会的灵感流经工人的血液，太阳之下再无更伟大的事；还有什么力量比个人的微弱力量更薄弱，因为工会使我们强大。

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:42.570
<v Ira Glass>There was "Glory Land," the official song for the 1994 World Cup, in which Darryl Hall, of and Oates fame, proclaimed to the soccer fans, "with passion rising high, you know that you can reach your goal."
<v Ira Glass>还有“荣耀之地”，1994年世界杯的官方歌曲，达里尔·霍尔（著名组合Hall & Oates成员）在歌中向足球迷们宣告，“激情高涨时，你知道你可以实现你的目标。”

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:44.080
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE GROANING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众呻吟]

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:45.230
<v Ira Glass>I know.
<v Ira Glass>我知道。

00:33:45.230 --> 00:33:47.870
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众笑声]

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:50.340
<v Ira Glass>And of course, that playground classic:
<v Ira Glass>当然，还有那个操场经典：

00:33:50.340 --> 00:34:17.140
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "PLAYGROUND VERSION OF 'THE BATTLE HYMN'" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA BAND] Mine eyes have seen the glory of the burning of the school, we have tortured every teacher, we have broken every rule; we have marched unto the principal to tell him he's a fool, the school is burning down. The school is burning down.
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”乐队的“操场版《战歌》”] 我的双眼见证了烧毁学校的荣光，我们折磨了每一位老师，我们打破了每一条规则；我们向校长行军告诉他他是傻瓜，学校正在燃烧。学校正在燃烧。

00:34:17.140 --> 00:34:26.560
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE CHEERING]
<v Ira Glass>[观众欢呼]

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:56.300
<v Ira Glass>In the 1950s, around the time of the Korean War, something else happened to Julia Ward How's "Battle Hymn of the Republic." A word changed. The line "let us die to make men free" got change to "let us live to make man free." The new wording acknowledged that sometimes war isn't always the answer. Plus, less of a downer.
<v Ira Glass>20世纪50年代，朝鲜战争时期，朱莉娅·沃德·豪的《共和国战歌》又发生了别的变化。一个词变了。“让我们牺牲以使人们自由”这一行改成了“让我们生活以使人类自由”。新的措辞承认战争并不总是答案。而且，没那么沮丧。

00:34:56.300 --> 00:35:21.860
<v Ira Glass>And through the years, a civil war of sorts has broken out between those who would die and those who would live. The Mormon Tabernacle Choir sings "live" on their 1959 Grammy Award-winning recording of the song. Joan Baez, leading a sing-along at a black college in Birmingham in 1962, went with the traditional "die."
<v Ira Glass>多年来，在那些愿意牺牲的人和那些愿意生活的人之间爆发了一场某种意义上的内战。摩门教大礼拜堂合唱团在他们1959年获得格莱美奖的录音中唱的是“生活”。琼·贝兹在1962年伯明翰的一所黑人大学带领合唱时，用了传统的“牺牲”。

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:58.660
<v Ira Glass>In my opinion, die is the way to go. When you get rid of the word, "die," you erase the most moving idea in the song, that if Christ died for us, we should be willing to die for each other. Why give that up? This the one clear-cut case where you can ask yourself, "what would Jesus do," and you know the answer. What would Jesus do? Die.
<v Ira Glass>在我看来，牺牲才是应该选的。当你去掉了“牺牲”这个词，你就抹去了这首歌中最动人的理念：如果基督为我们牺牲，我们也应该愿意为彼此牺牲。为什么要放弃这个？这是一个你可以问自己“耶稣会怎么做”的明确案例，而且你知道答案。耶稣会怎么做？牺牲。

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:35.560
<v Ira Glass>And, of course, people disagree about the wording. We disagree about everything else in this country. And when we sing the song today, I think we even disagree about who the serpents are that our heels should crush. Some of us see our enemies abroad, some see them at home, some of us are still mad at France. And that's the way it's been with this song all through its history. It gets adapted for one use, than another. The melody is so powerful, the words so strong, you feel like you really would lay down your life for a just cause. Of your choosing.
<v Ira Glass>当然，人们对措辞有分歧。我们在这个国家的其他所有事情上都有分歧。今天当我们唱这首歌时，我想我们甚至对我们要粉碎的蛇是谁都有分歧。我们中的一些人看到了国外的敌人，一些人在国内看到了敌人，还有些人仍然对法国感到恼火。这就是这首歌在整个历史中的样子。它被改编用于一种用途，然后是另一种。旋律是如此强大，歌词是如此有力，你会觉得你真的会为正义事业贡献你的生命。你所选择的那种。

00:36:35.560 --> 00:37:23.840
<v Ira Glass>When we sing "The Battle Hymn"-- and I say we because that is how the song is traditionally performed at public events, as a sing-along in which a group of citizens turn themselves into a choir-- we sing about taking action, about marching on, about doing something. And this is the best part about singing "The Battle Hymn:" you're not standing there alone doing something, you're part of something. The song starts off with "mine eyes," and "I have seen," and by the end it's "you and me" and "let us die" or "let us live," whatever-- "us" being the point. We're all in it together, if only for the length of the song.
<v Ira Glass>当我们唱《战歌》时——我说“我们”，因为这是这首歌在公共活动中作为集体演唱的传统表演方式，一群公民把自己变成了一个合唱团——我们唱的是采取行动，关于前进，关于做点什么。而这是唱《战歌》最好的一点：你不是独自一人站在那里做某事，你是某事的一部分。歌曲开始于“我的双眼”和“我已见证”，到最后变成了“你和我”和“让我们牺牲”或“让我们生活”，随它吧——重点是“我们”。我们都在一起，哪怕只是在那首歌的时间里。

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:33.310
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell. She's the author of several books, including The Partly Cloudy Patriot.
<v Ira Glass>萨拉·沃威尔。她是包括《部分多云的爱国者》在内的几本书的作者。

00:37:33.310 --> 00:38:22.590
<v Ira Glass>Word about the band that's here in the stage. On this tour, we have the band OK Go. And we have this bad, headed by the seme-legendary Jon Langford of the Mekons in the Waco Brothers. A while back, John did a story with us and producer Starlee Kine on our staff in our show about the classified ads, we he put together an entire band from the musicians classifieds. And he's brought two of those musicians from that story with him here on the tour. On the far end you'll see Eric Muller on the theremin. Eric used to work for a company that made parts for the railroad. And on this end is Nathan Swanson, who still works part time for Home Depot, playing electric violin and mandolin. Alan on bass and Dan on drums, they're old pros.
<v Ira Glass>说说在舞台上的乐队。在这次巡演中，我们有OK Go乐队。我们还有这支由来自Mekons乐队和Waco Brothers乐队、半传奇人物乔恩·兰福德领衔的乐队。前段时间，乔恩和我们制作组的斯塔莉·凯恩一起做了一个关于分类广告的故事，他把分类广告里的音乐家组织成了一支完整的乐队。他把故事里的那两名音乐家也带到了巡演中。在最远端你可以看到埃里克·穆勒在弹奏特雷门琴。埃里克曾经在一家生产铁路零件的公司工作。在这一端是内森·斯旺森，他还在家得宝兼职工作，演奏电小提琴和曼陀林。艾伦担任贝斯手，丹担任鼓手，他们都是老手。

00:38:22.590 --> 00:40:38.990
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "HELL'S ROOF" BY JON LANGFORD AND THE LOST IN AMERICA TOUR BAND]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 乔恩·兰福德和“迷失在美国”巡演乐队的“地狱之顶”]

00:40:38.990 --> 00:40:43.490
<v Ira Glass>That was great.
<v Ira Glass>太棒了。

00:40:43.490 --> 00:42:00.820
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, scraps of paper, personal letters, handmade signs, all discarded and then found. Maybe there's something of yours in there. You'll find out in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，纸片、私人信件、手工标语，全部被丢弃然后被找回。也许里面有你的东西。一会儿你就会知道。来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:23.010
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week, of course, we choose theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, taped on the road in Boston, Portland, Oregon, Denver, Chicago, and Washington DC: Lost in America. Stories of people who are lost, words that are lost, things that are lost, and, now and again, found.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来围绕该主题的各种故事。今天的节目在波士顿、俄勒冈州波特兰、丹佛、芝加哥和华盛顿特区的路演中录制：《迷失在美国》。讲述关于迷失的人、迷失的文字、迷失的事物，以及偶尔被找回的故事。

00:42:23.010 --> 00:42:27.780
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three: I Found Your Letter.
<v Ira Glass>我们到达了节目的第三幕。第三幕：我找到了你的信。

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:43.430
<v Ira Glass>You know when you're walking down the street and you spot some random piece of paper floating around-- a page from a letter, a shopping list with a note on it, some kid's paper from class-- do you pick it up? Do you read it? Me? Very nosy, yes.
<v Ira Glass>你知道当你走在街上，发现一张随处飘荡的随机纸片——一封信的一页，写着备注的购物清单，某个孩子班级的作业纸——你会捡起来吗？你会读吗？我？非常好奇，是的。

00:42:43.430 --> 00:42:53.610
<v Ira Glass>As writing goes, these pieces of paper, they're little writing orphans, cut off from their original life, cut off from everything that gave them meaning. But still there with a lot on their minds and a lot to say.
<v Ira Glass>文字作品来说，这些纸片是小的文字孤儿，与它们的原始生活切断，与赋予它们意义的一切切断。但它们仍然在那里，心中装着很多东西，有很多话要说。

00:42:53.610 --> 00:43:15.430
<v Ira Glass>A few years back, Davy Rothbart decided that somebody, somewhere should do a magazine that was made up entirely of this kind of writing. And then through sheer force of will, he became that somebody and created Found Magazine. Now, people all over the country send him this stuff. He's going to come out and read some of this writing that has been discarded and misplaced, lost by its original owners.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，戴维·罗斯巴特决定，有人、在某处应该做一本完全由这种文字组成的杂志。然后凭借纯粹的意志力，他成了那个人，并创办了《发现》杂志。现在，全国各地的人们把这些东西寄给他。他要出来读一些被丢弃和放错位置、被其最初拥有者遗失的文字。

00:43:15.430 --> 00:43:17.820
<v Ira Glass>Please welcome Davy Rothbart.
<v Ira Glass>请欢迎戴维·罗斯巴特。

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:25.590
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>[观众鼓掌]

00:43:25.590 --> 00:43:38.760
<v Davy Rothbart>Everyone can find stuff. And I want to give you just a sense of some of the stuff that people are finding and sending in to us. This right here is a note that was found in LA.
<v Davy Rothbart>每个人都能发现东西。我想让你感受一下人们正在发现并寄给我们的东西。这正是一张在洛杉矶发现的便条。

00:43:38.760 --> 00:44:01.800
<v Davy Rothbart>And it says, "Jesse, I did not take anything. I know there's no convincing you once you've made up your mind. And although I cannot offer you any other explanation as to what happened to it, that doesn't mean I did it. How could I have? You say your car was locked and Katie had the keys.
<v Davy Rothbart>它写着，“杰西，我什么也没拿。我知道一旦你拿定主意，就没法说服你了。虽然我无法对它发生了什么给你任何其他的解释，但这并不意味着是我做的。我怎么可能做呢？你说你的车锁了，而凯蒂有钥匙。

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:17.400
<v Davy Rothbart>Anyway, I don't need to take something of yours when I could get my own. It doesn't make sense. But here's a replacement, because I can't stand when you think I've wronged you. Mom."
<v Davy Rothbart>无论如何，当我能自己搞到东西时，我不需要拿你的。这说不通。但这儿有一个替代品，因为我受不了你以为我冤枉了你。妈。”

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:21.530
<v Davy Rothbart>[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
<v Davy Rothbart>[观众笑声]

00:44:21.530 --> 00:44:33.200
<v Davy Rothbart>The authors of some of these notes, they don't always paint themselves in the best light, you know? Sometimes they're going fine, fine, and they just skewer themselves at the end. And this is one that was found in Tacoma, Washington.
<v Davy Rothbart>这些便条的作者，他们并不总是把自己写得很好，你知道吗？有时他们开头写得好好的，到最后却把自己给刺穿了。这是一张在华盛顿州塔科马发现的便条。

00:44:33.200 --> 00:45:05.120
<v Davy Rothbart>And at the top, OK, it just says, "what kind of relationship do I want? How do I see myself in it? I'm nurturing, I'm relied upon, I'm getting my ya-yas." But then down here it says, "what I hate about women: they're hard, impossible to please and satisfy. They talk a lot without saying much or being intimate. And they're self-involved and self-absorbed. I want them to be absorbed with me."
<v Davy Rothbart>在顶部，好吧，它只写着，“我想要什么样的关系？我如何在其中看待自己？我是培养性的，我是被依赖的，我正在获得我的快乐。”但下面写着，“我讨厌女人的地方：她们很难，不可能取悦和满足。她们说很多话却什么也没说，也不亲密。而且她们以自我为中心，只关心自己。我希望她们关心我。”

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:22.230
<v Davy Rothbart>This one was found in Berkeley, California, actually on the front lawn of a fraternity house. And it was a whole book of letters back and forth from the elders of the house to the pledges, the young kids who are trying to get into the fraternity. So this is one of the notes that's from one of the elders to one of the young pledges named A.J.
<v Davy Rothbart>这张是在加州伯克利发现的，实际上是在一个兄弟会房子的前草坪上。那是整整一本书的信件，从房子的长辈来回写给准会员，那些试图进入兄弟会的年轻人。所以这是其中一张便条，从其中一位长辈写给名叫A.J.的年轻准会员的。

00:45:22.230 --> 00:46:12.120
<v Davy Rothbart>And he says, "A.J., tonight's event was just a tradition of the house. It was not gay. Even though you had to pull down your pants, at least you didn't have to show it to all the actives, just your pledge master. Its meaning: to prove your manhood and that you are not a boy. But none of us are gay. Aside from all this, I want to know that I've seen some improvement out of you since your first pledge. Keep up the effort and have a positive attitude of things. I tell ya, it's all worth it once you've crossed. Take my word for it. Just hang in there, OK?
<v Davy Rothbart>他说，“A.J.，今晚的活动只是房子的传统。这不是同性恋。即使你不得不脱掉裤子，至少你不必展示给所有正式成员看，只要给你的入会指导者看就行。它的意义：证明你的男子气概，证明你不是个男孩。但我们中没有一个是同性恋。撇开所有这些，我想知道，自你第一次成为准会员以来，我确实看到你有所进步。保持努力，对事物持积极态度。我告诉你，一旦你跨过那一关，一切都是值得的。相信我。坚持下去，好吗？

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:26.280
<v Davy Rothbart>Listen, I want you to have dinner with me when you're free someday. It's a great chance for you to get to know me better and for me to answer questions you may have, all right? Aaron."
<v Davy Rothbart>听着，我希望你哪天有空时和我一起吃晚饭。这是你更好地了解我，以及我回答你可能有的问题的绝佳机会，好吗？亚伦。”

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:53.450
<v Davy Rothbart>And so many of the notes, as I read through them when they come in the mail every day that people are finding, they deal with love and relationships, you know? And one of the favorite notes that I've ever found myself is this note I found in Chicago on my windshield. I came out of my friend's apartment late one night. It had snowed a little bit. And you know, with like a thin layer of snow, every car looks a little bit the same. And my name's Davy, but there's a note on my windshield that was addressed to Marco.
<v Davy Rothbart>每天邮件中寄来的这么多便条，人们发现的，它们都处理爱情和关系，你知道吗？我曾经发现过的最喜欢的便条之一，是我在芝加哥的车窗上发现的。一天深夜，我从朋友公寓出来。下了一点雪。你知道，那一层薄薄的雪，每辆车看起来都有点一样。我的名字叫戴维，但车窗上的便条是写给马可的。

00:46:53.450 --> 00:47:19.690
<v Davy Rothbart>And it said, "Marco, I freakin' hate you. You said you had to work? Then why is your car here at her place? You're a freakin' liar. I hate you, I freakin' hate you. Crystal. P.S. Page me later?"
<v Davy Rothbart>上面写着，“马可，我他妈讨厌你。你说你要工作？那为什么你的车在她那儿？你是个他妈的骗子。我讨厌你，我他妈讨厌你。克里斯特尔。附言：之后传呼我？”

00:47:19.690 --> 00:47:42.840
<v Davy Rothbart>And then this one just came in last week from-- it was found in North Carolina. And it says, "Dear Will, the longer I think about what I'm doing, the sicker I feel. Will, I'm sorry, but I don't think we should continue to have a relationship together. At least not as a couple. I love you, but things have not been the same since we found out that we were related."
<v Davy Rothbart>还有这一张是上周刚收到的——它在北卡罗来纳州被发现。它写着，“亲爱的威尔，我越是思考我在做什么，我就越觉得恶心。威尔，对不起，但我认为我们不应该继续保持关系了。至少作为情侣不应该。我爱你，但自从我们发现我们有亲戚关系以来，事情就不一样了。”

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:50.210
<v Davy Rothbart>[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
<v Davy Rothbart>[观众笑声]

00:47:50.210 --> 00:48:20.410
<v Davy Rothbart>My little brother, Peter, he is the best finder I know. He amazes me with what he brings home. And this is one he found when he was visiting me in Chicago. And it's written in like a kid's handwriting. It looks like maybe a 12-year-old kid. It says, "To cashier, will you please sell my son one pack of Newport Lights." Ah, that trick. "Thank you for your help." On there's just this scrawled, adult-looking signature.
<v Davy Rothbart>我的弟弟彼得，他是我认识的最好的发现者。他带回家的东西让我震惊。这是他在芝加哥探望我时发现的一张。它是用孩子的笔迹写的。看起来像是一个12岁左右的孩子。上面写着，“给收银员，请卖给我儿子一包新港淡烟。”啊，那个诡计。“谢谢你的帮助。”上面只有一个乱糟糟、看起来像成年人的签名。

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:31.990
<v Davy Rothbart>And then this one my brother found pinned under the back tire of his bicycle. And it's this handsome looking sign. Someone spent a good deal of time putting this thing together.
<v Davy Rothbart>还有这一张是我弟弟在他的自行车后轮胎下发现的。这是一个看起来很帅气的标语。有人花了很多时间做这个东西。

00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:53.030
<v Davy Rothbart>It says, "After leaving the building please lock this door. It will prevent unauthorized people from entering the building and defecating in the washing machine. Many thanks."
<v Davy Rothbart>上面写着，“离开大楼后请锁好这扇门。这将防止未经授权的人进入大楼并在洗衣机里排便。非常感谢。”

00:48:53.030 --> 00:49:13.670
<v Davy Rothbart>And when you send your finds in, I ask that you name your finds. And also that you write one line, you know, a sense of response or interpretation to it. About this one, my brother wrote, he says, "Note that those who are authorized to defecate in the washing machine will be given a key for entry."
<v Davy Rothbart>当你把你的发现寄来时，我要求你给你的发现命名。而且你还要写下一行，你知道，一种回应或解释。关于这一张，我弟弟写道，他说，“请注意，那些被授权在洗衣机里排便的人将获得一把入口钥匙。”

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:36.210
<v Davy Rothbart>And yeah, I applaud for whoever made that sign, but also for my brother, who just picked it up off the ground. And I asked him, you know, "how do you do it? What's your secret?" My brother says that it's been scientifically calculated-- by him-- that one in five, 20%, of all finds are gems of finds. So that means if you pick one thing up a day, that every week you'll have a real jewel of a find.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，我为制作那个标语的人鼓掌，也为我弟弟鼓掌，他只是把它从地上捡了起来。我问他，“你怎么做到的？你的秘密是什么？”我弟弟说经过科学计算——由他自己计算——所有的发现中，有五分之一，即20%是极品。这意味着如果你每天捡一样东西，每周你就会有一个真正的珠宝般的发现。

00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:46.770
<v Davy Rothbart>Let's read this one here. This is from Erie, Pennsylvania. And it's a note, a kid writing a letter to his dad.
<v Davy Rothbart>读一下这个。这是来自宾夕法尼亚州伊利的。这是一张便条，一个孩子写给他爸爸的信。

00:49:46.770 --> 00:50:06.470
<v Davy Rothbart>It says, "Hi, Dad. What's up? Not much here. Just working to save money. I'm working at Old Country Buffet starting at $6.50 an hour cooking. Dad, are there any cooking jobs down there? Let me know when you write me back. I gave Cleav your letter, OK? Without Mom knowing, OK?
<v Davy Rothbart>上面写着，“嘿，爸爸。你好吗？我这儿没什么。就是打工攒钱。我在老乡村自助餐打工，时薪6.50美元负责做饭。爸爸，那儿有做饭的工作吗？回信的时候告诉我。我把你的信给克里夫了，好吗？别让妈妈知道，好吗？

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:24.950
<v Davy Rothbart>I can't wait to come down there. Erie sucks bad. There ain't nothing to do. Too cold. The weather sucks, too. Dad, I drink, too. Beer. Budweiser. But I don't drink a lot. And I do a little drugs, too. I smoke joints. And I got a bowl that's cool. I'll let you see it, OK? I talked to Grandma. She said she's going to send you a letter and some money.
<v Davy Rothbart>我等不及要下去了。伊利太烂了。没啥可做的。太冷了。天气也烂透了。爸爸，我也喝酒。啤酒。百威。但我喝得不多。我也玩点毒品。我抽大麻烟。而且我有个很酷的烟锅。我会让你看看的，好吗？我和祖母谈过了。她说她会给你寄封信和一些钱。

00:50:24.950 --> 00:50:31.600
<v Davy Rothbart>Dad, I don't know if Cleav or Beth is coming down, but I'll talk to them and see. If not, it will be just me only.
<v Davy Rothbart>爸爸，我不知道克里夫或贝丝是否会下去，但我会和他们谈谈看。如果不去，就只有我一个人。

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:54.470
<v Davy Rothbart>Dad, you're going to rent a mobile home. That's cheap. $100 a week. That's cheap. Dad, try and see if there's any apartments for rent and let me know how much, OK? Dad, I'm going to take a Greyhound because it's cheap-- only $49-- but I'll call you ahead of time. Dad, all the money I'm saving from working at my job and my income tax check, I should have a lot, OK? With the money, we should be able to get a place for us, OK?
<v Davy Rothbart>爸爸，你要租个移动房屋。那很便宜。每周100美元。那很便宜。爸爸，试着看看有没有公寓出租，告诉我多少钱，好吗？爸爸，我打算坐灰狗巴士，因为它很便宜——只要49美元——但我会提前给你打电话。爸爸，我从打工攒下的钱和所得税退款，我应该会攒很多，好吗？有了钱，我们应该能搞到住的地方，好吗？

00:50:54.470 --> 00:50:56.970
<v Davy Rothbart>Dad, I can't wait to come down there pretty soon."
<v Davy Rothbart>爸爸，我等不及要很快下去了。”

00:50:56.970 --> 00:50:59.120
<v Davy Rothbart>Turn over.
<v Davy Rothbart>翻面。

00:50:59.120 --> 00:54:56.720
<v Davy Rothbart>"Dad, I'm a cook, and I can cook us some [BLEEP] up. Cook some meals up for us, get a grill and cook on that. Also, I got a lot of CDs, like AC/DC, Led Zeppelin, Metallica, Pink Floyd, The Doors, Jimi Hendrix, Judas Priest, Black Sabbath, and all that. We can jam out and have lots of fun.
<v Davy Rothbart>“爸爸，我是个厨师，我可以给我们做点烂东西吃。做点饭，弄个烤架烤着吃。另外，我有很多CD，像AC/DC，齐柏林飞艇，金属，平克·弗洛伊德，大门，吉米·亨德里克斯，犹大圣徒，黑色安息日，等等。我们可以一起玩，玩得很开心。

00:54:56.720 --> 00:56:44.900
<v Ira Glass>Davy Rothbart. You can go to his magazine's website, foundmagazine.com.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·罗斯巴特。你可以访问他的杂志网站，foundmagazine.com。

00:56:44.900 --> 00:57:03.840
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Julie Snyder, Diane Cook, Todd Bachmann, me, with our other producers, Alex Blumberg, Wendy Dorr, and Starlee Kine. Production help from Katie O'Dunn. Engineering for our show in Boston by Miles Smith. In DC, Jonathan Cherry and Big Mo Recording. In Portland, John Frazy. In Chicago, Mary Gaffney. Our March guru, Jorge Just.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由朱莉·斯奈德、黛安·库克、托德·巴克曼和我制作，我们的其他制片人还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、温迪·多尔和斯塔莉·凯恩。凯蒂·奥丹提供了制作帮助。波士顿节目的工程由迈尔斯·史密斯负责。在华盛顿，由乔纳森·切里和Big Mo Recording负责。在波特兰，由约翰·弗雷齐负责。在芝加哥，由玛丽·加夫尼负责。我们的选曲大师是乔治·贾斯特。

00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:22.680
<v Ira Glass>Thanks to Adam Beckman. Ken Sleuter mixed one of our bands. Brand Cutsman the other. Thanks also to Robbie Willard. Thanks to the public radio stations who have been our hosts and presenters in the five cities. WBUR in Boston. WAMU, 88.5 FM in DC. Oregon Public Broadcasting in Portland. Colorado Public Radio in Denver. And Chicago Public Radio in our hometown.
